# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Multi-School Management System — PRODUCTION environment template
# SRS §27 "Environment Variables". Checklist row 7.5.
#
# This is the production counterpart of `backend/.env.example`. Sections, key
# order and key names are identical to that file on purpose, so a key added to
# one is meant to be visibly missing from the other.
#
# Do NOT check that with a plain `diff` of the two files. `.env.example` is 124
# lines with sparse comments; this file is several times that and almost all of it
# is prose, so `diff` reports essentially the whole file as changed and a missing
# key is invisible in the output. Compare the KEY SETS instead:
#
#     grep -oE '^[A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*=' backend/.env.example > /tmp/a
#     grep -oE '^[A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*=' deploy/env/production.env.example > /tmp/b
#     sort -o /tmp/a /tmp/a && sort -o /tmp/b /tmp/b && diff /tmp/a /tmp/b
#
# That prints nothing as this file ships: 73 keys on each side, identical names.
# It was run, not assumed.
#
# ## Where this file goes
#
# `src/config/env.js:14` sets ROOT to the *backend* directory and `env.js:17-23`
# loads exactly `<ROOT>/.env` when NODE_ENV is anything but `test`. There is no
# per-environment file name and no `--env-file` switch. So:
#
#     cp deploy/env/production.env.example backend/.env
#     chmod 600 backend/.env         # it holds every secret the system has
#     chown <the PM2 user> backend/.env
#
# Naming it `production.env` on the server does nothing — env.js will not read it.
#
# ## How values are parsed
#
# `dotenv.config()` is called plain (env.js:22) with no dotenv-expand, so values
# are LITERAL: `$OTHER` and `${OTHER}` are not expanded, and an unquoted `#`
# starts a comment. A password containing `#` MUST be double-quoted or it will be
# silently truncated at that character.
#
# The same plain call has a second consequence that bites under PM2: there is no
# `override: true`, so dotenv does NOT overwrite a variable already present in the
# process environment. Anything PM2 injects — an `env: { ... }` block in the
# ecosystem file — or anything exported in the launching shell BEATS this file
# silently. It matters most for NODE_ENV, whose five armed behaviours (env.js:66,
# :279-289) all key off whichever value actually won. Either leave a key out of the
# PM2 process definition entirely or make sure the two agree; do not set it in both
# and assume this file is authoritative.
#
# Three keys are in exactly that state as `deploy/pm2/ecosystem.config.js` ships,
# so check them against this file rather than assuming:
#   - NODE_ENV=production, injected for BOTH apps (ecosystem.config.js:349 and
#     :500). It agrees with this file, so nothing is lost — but it is PM2's value
#     that arrives, not this one;
#   - ENABLE_CRON=true, injected for the cron app only (ecosystem.config.js:501);
#   - LOG_DIR=storage/logs/cron, injected for the cron app only
#     (ecosystem.config.js:502). This one DISAGREES with the LOG_DIR set below by
#     design, and the consequence is written out at that key's entry.
#
# Every numeric key here is parsed by `num()` (env.js:38-46), which THROWS on a
# non-number at module load — before `listen`, before the database connection,
# before anything. A typo in a number is therefore a refused boot naming the key
# ("Environment variable X must be a number, received ..."), not a degraded
# runtime. That covers twenty keys — every one of them: PORT, DB_PORT,
# DB_POOL_MAX/MIN, BCRYPT_ROUNDS, PASSWORD_RESET_TOKEN_TTL_MINUTES,
# EMAIL_VERIFICATION_TOKEN_TTL_HOURS, PASSWORD_MIN_LENGTH, LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS,
# LOGIN_LOCKOUT_MINUTES, the four rate-limit numbers (RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MINUTES,
# RATE_LIMIT_MAX, AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX, AI_RATE_LIMIT_MAX), MAX_UPLOAD_MB,
# CACHE_TTL_SECONDS, QUEUE_CONCURRENCY, MAIL_PORT, LOG_RETENTION_DAYS and
# BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS. The count is mechanical: `num(` appears against exactly
# those twenty names in env.js. An EMPTY value is not a typo — it falls back to the
# default (env.js:40).
#
# `str()` (env.js:28-35) treats an EMPTY value exactly like an unset one and falls
# back to the default. That is deliberate and is used below: twelve values ship
# empty. Do not "fill in" any of them with dummy text — a 32-character placeholder
# that does not begin with `change-me` passes every check in env.js and would ship
# a guessable signing key.
#
# Be precise about which of those twelve the boot actually defends, because the
# answer is three. `assertRuntimeConfig()` (env.js:265-294, called from
# `src/server.js:86` before `listen`) enforces JWT_ACCESS_SECRET and
# JWT_REFRESH_SECRET (env.js:268-276) and ANTHROPIC_API_KEY when AI_DRIVER is
# `anthropic` (env.js:286-288). Leave those empty and the process refuses to start,
# which is exactly the loud failure this file is buying.
#
# The other nine are NOT asserted anywhere. REDIS_URL is meant to stay empty — see
# CACHE_DRIVER. The remaining eight are yours to fill: DB_PASSWORD,
# SUPER_ADMIN_PASSWORD, MAIL_HOST, MAIL_USER, MAIL_PASSWORD and the three
# ONLINE_GATEWAY_* secrets. Each of their entries below states what happens if you
# do not, and in two cases the answer is "the system comes up and looks fine". Do
# not read "empty" as "the boot will catch it".
#
# Know the boundary of that protection: `src/server.js:86` is the ONLY call site of
# `assertRuntimeConfig()` in the repository (grep confirms it — env.js:265 defines
# it, env.js:297 exports it, server.js:86 calls it, and nothing else). It guards
# the API process and nothing else. `npm run cron` (src/jobs/cron.js) requires
# `../config/env` at cron.js:65 and never asserts, so the scheduler will happily
# start against an unfilled .env. See ENABLE_CRON below.
#
# ## Generating the secrets
#
# Run each command separately, once per secret, and paste the output. Never reuse
# one value for two keys; env.js:274-276 rejects an access secret equal to the
# refresh secret, and two independent draws are the point.
#
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
#
# or, equivalently:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 48
#
# 48 bytes yields a 64-character string, comfortably over the 32-character floor
# that env.js:268 and env.js:271 enforce. Every key marked `<<< GENERATE >>>`
# below is one of these.
#
# ## Anything not listed here
#
# Every key env.js reads is present in this file — all 73, verified against
# `.env.example` (73 keys; the two sets are identical, no key on either side is
# missing from the other). Seventy-two of those arrive through the
# `str()`/`num()`/`bool()`/`list()` helpers; the seventy-third is NODE_ENV, which
# env.js reads straight from `process.env` at :18 and :25. A grep for the helper
# calls alone therefore counts 72 and looks one short — it is not.
#
# Adding a key that env.js does not read achieves nothing: nothing enumerates the
# environment, so a typo'd name is not an error, it is silence. See the notes at
# the foot of this file for the three keys that env.js reads but no other code
# consumes.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── Application ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# MUST DIFFER FROM DEV (`development`). This one variable arms five separate
# behaviours; getting it wrong is not cosmetic:
#   - `secure: true` on the refresh cookie (src/modules/auth/auth.controller.js:65,
#     :76) and on the CSRF cookie (src/middlewares/csrf.js:57). Over plain HTTP the
#     browser then discards both and every login silently fails to persist — which
#     is why the TLS termination in front of this app is not optional.
#   - the console log transport is DROPPED (src/config/logger.js:44). `pm2 logs`
#     will show nothing from the application; the record lives only in LOG_DIR.
#   - the placeholder-secret and ANTHROPIC_API_KEY checks turn on (env.js:279-289).
#   - destructive database CLI commands refuse to run (src/database/cli.js:42).
#   - the Super Admin seeder throws instead of warning on the example password
#     (src/database/seeders/04-super-admin.js:64-70).
NODE_ENV=production

# Unchanged from dev. nginx proxies to this port on the loopback interface.
# `app.listen(config.app.port)` (src/server.js:99) is called WITHOUT a bind
# address, so the process listens on ALL interfaces. nginx in front of it does
# not prevent a direct request to <public-ip>:4000 that bypasses TLS, the access
# log and any nginx-level limit. There is no HOST/BIND key to fix this — close
# the port at the host firewall.
PORT=4000

# Unchanged. Baked into the refresh cookie's `path` (auth.controller.js:51), so
# changing it after users hold cookies orphans every existing session.
API_PREFIX=/api/v1

# Unchanged. Appears in the startup line (server.js:101) and nowhere security-relevant.
APP_NAME=Multi-School Management System

# MUST DIFFER FROM DEV (`http://localhost:4000`) — set it to the public HTTPS
# origin of the API.
# Stated plainly: env.js:107 reads this into `config.app.url` and NOTHING in
# `src/` reads `config.app.url`. Setting it correctly changes no behaviour today;
# it is kept accurate so that the first consumer (an absolute link in an email, a
# webhook callback URL) is not built from a stale localhost value.
APP_URL=https://api.msms.example.com

# MUST DIFFER FROM DEV (`http://localhost:3000`). This is NOT decorative: it is
# string-concatenated into the password-reset and email-verification links that
# are mailed to users (src/modules/auth/auth.service.js:488 and :696). Left at
# localhost, every reset email sent to a real user is a dead link; set to `http://`
# on a public host, it mails a one-time credential over cleartext.
FRONTEND_URL=https://app.msms.example.com

# MUST DIFFER FROM DEV. Comma-separated exact origins — scheme + host + port, no
# path, no trailing slash, no wildcard. `credentials: true` is fixed in
# src/app.js:193 because the refresh cookie requires it, and the CORS spec forbids
# `*` alongside credentials; src/app.js:183 does an exact `Array.includes` match,
# so `https://app.msms.example.com` and `https://app.msms.example.com/` are different
# strings and one of them will be refused with a 403 (app.js:186-191).
# Include every browser origin that calls the API — the Next.js frontend, and a
# separate admin host if one exists. Do NOT add the API's own origin.
CORS_ORIGINS=https://app.msms.example.com

# Unchanged. Applied to BOTH express.json and express.urlencoded (src/app.js:270-271),
# and that is the whole of its reach: those two parsers only claim
# `application/json` and `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`. A multipart upload is
# NOT bounded by this key at any size — that is MAX_UPLOAD_MB's job, further down.
# nginx's `client_max_body_size` therefore has to be sized from the upload ceiling,
# not from this number; the derivation is in the MAX_UPLOAD_MB block. If nginx is
# set below the figure derived there, it returns its own HTML 413 and the caller
# never sees the application's JSON error with its limit details (upload.js:143-146).
JSON_BODY_LIMIT=100kb


# ── Database (SRS §3 — MySQL) ────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Keep 127.0.0.1 when MySQL runs on the same host as the API — the loopback
# interface keeps the credential and the wire traffic off the network entirely.
# Use the literal `127.0.0.1`, not `localhost`: on Linux, `localhost` makes the
# MySQL client prefer a unix socket, and the socket path is not configurable here.
DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
DB_PORT=3306
DB_NAME=msms

# MUST DIFFER FROM DEV (`root`). Create a dedicated account for this application.
# THREE consumers share it, and the strictest of the three sets the floor. Read
# this before the first deploy — "read/write" is NOT enough and the failure lands
# on the very first command:
#
#   1. THE MIGRATION CLI NEEDS DDL. `npm run db:migrate` (src/database/cli.js:71-76)
#      runs the single migration in this repository,
#      src/database/migrations/20260825120000-initial-schema.js, which calls
#      `queryInterface.createTable` (:30), `addIndex` (:36) and `addConstraint`
#      (:46) to build the whole schema. That needs CREATE, ALTER, INDEX and
#      REFERENCES. An account granted only SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE fails on
#      the first CREATE TABLE — i.e. before the API has ever been started.
#      `migrate` also carries no production guard: `assertNotProduction`
#      (cli.js:41-45) is called by `drop` (:61), `migrate:undo` (:79), `seed:undo`
#      (:111), `seed:demo` (:123), `seed:demo:undo` (:131) and `reset` (:139), and
#      NOT by `migrate` (:71). It will run here, which is the intent.
#   2. THE API needs ordinary read/write for the life of the deployment.
#   3. `mysqldump` IN THE BACKUP TASK (spawned at
#      src/jobs/tasks/databaseBackup.js:69, argv at :53-62) needs SELECT plus
#      SHOW VIEW, TRIGGER and EXECUTE for its `--routines --triggers`, all of
#      which are database-scopable and so covered by the grant below — AND one
#      privilege that CANNOT be scoped to a database, which the grant below
#      therefore does not confer. See MYSQLDUMP_PATH, and do not skip it or the
#      nightly backup fails on a server nobody is watching.
#
# The smallest grant covering (1), (2) and everything in (3) that can be scoped to
# a database, issued once as an administrator:
#
#     CREATE DATABASE msms CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;
#     CREATE USER 'msms'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY '<the DB_PASSWORD below>';
#     GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON msms.* TO 'msms'@'localhost';
#     FLUSH PRIVILEGES;
#
# `ALL PRIVILEGES ON msms.*` is database-scoped — it confers every privilege that
# can be limited to one database and none that cannot, so this is not a server
# administrator. Creating the database by hand above also means `npm run db:create`
# (cli.js:47-58) is a no-op if anyone runs it: it issues
# `CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS` with the same charset and collation.
#
# Narrowing the grant further is legitimate, but enumerate it against the list
# above rather than against the word "read/write", and re-check it whenever a
# second migration is added.
DB_USER=msms

# <<< REQUIRED — no generation command, this is the password you set on the MySQL
# account above. >>>
# Left empty on purpose so it is obviously unfilled. WARNING, and this is the trap:
# `assertRuntimeConfig()` checks DB_NAME (env.js:277) but NEVER checks DB_PASSWORD.
# An empty password here does not stop the boot — it just tries to connect without
# one, and if the MySQL account also has no password the whole system comes up
# fully functional and completely open. Quote the value if it contains `#`.
DB_PASSWORD=

# Unchanged in value, but understand what it means here: this is the pool size
# PER NODE PROCESS. Under PM2 the API and the cron process each build their own
# pool, so the connection ceiling is 15 × (number of node processes), and that
# total must stay below the server's `max_connections`. Raising PM2 instances
# without raising `max_connections` fails as intermittent "Too many connections"
# under load, not at startup.
DB_POOL_MAX=15
DB_POOL_MIN=0

# Keep false. `true` makes Sequelize write every statement — including the bound
# parameter values, so student records and password hashes — into the winston
# files under LOG_DIR, which are then kept for LOG_RETENTION_DAYS.
DB_LOGGING=false

# Keep +00:00. The application stores and compares timestamps in UTC; a server
# whose MySQL session offset differs makes the cron sweeps (subscription expiry,
# invoice overdue) fire against the wrong day boundary.
DB_TIMEZONE=+00:00


# ── Test database (used when NODE_ENV=test) ──────────────────────────────────

# Inert in production. env.js:125 only consults DB_NAME_TEST when NODE_ENV is
# `test`; with NODE_ENV=production the value is never read. Kept so this file and
# `.env.example` stay diffable, and so nobody "fixes" its absence by adding it back
# with a wrong name.
DB_NAME_TEST=msms_test


# ── JWT (SRS §7, §24 "JWT Security") ─────────────────────────────────────────

# <<< GENERATE — both, separately. >>>
# Left EMPTY rather than given a placeholder. Empty means env.js:135-136 falls
# back to `''` outside tests, and assertRuntimeConfig (env.js:268-273) aborts the
# boot with "must be set and at least 32 characters long" before the port is
# bound. A placeholder string long enough to pass that check would instead boot
# happily with a key an attacker can guess — the failure mode this emptiness buys
# is loud, and the alternative is silent.
#
# Three rules env.js actually enforces, so there is no point arguing with them:
#   1. each at least 32 characters (env.js:268, :271);
#   2. the two must not be equal (env.js:274-276) — otherwise a refresh token is
#      a valid access token and the 15-minute access lifetime means nothing;
#   3. neither may start with `change-me` when NODE_ENV=production (env.js:280-285).
#
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Rotating either value invalidates every token signed with the old one, logging
# every user out. That is the intended emergency response to a leaked .env.
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=

# Unchanged. The short access lifetime is what makes a stolen access token
# survivable; the refresh cookie is httpOnly, SameSite=Lax and path-scoped to
# `${API_PREFIX}/auth` (auth.controller.js:50-70) to limit where it is sent.
JWT_ACCESS_EXPIRES_IN=15m
JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRES_IN=7d

# Unchanged. Both are verified on every token, so changing either in place logs
# out every existing session — the same blast radius as rotating a secret, with
# none of the benefit. Change them only alongside a deliberate rotation.
JWT_ISSUER=msms
JWT_AUDIENCE=msms-api


# ── Passwords ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Unchanged. 12 is the production value; env.js:145 already drops to 4 under test
# only. Raising it costs CPU on every login and every password change, which on a
# single-core VPS is a self-inflicted denial of service under a login flood —
# AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX below is what actually bounds that.
BCRYPT_ROUNDS=12
PASSWORD_RESET_TOKEN_TTL_MINUTES=60
EMAIL_VERIFICATION_TOKEN_TTL_HOURS=48

# Unchanged. Length only, no composition rule — the reasoning is at env.js:148-154
# and it is deliberate, not an oversight. Raise the number if policy demands it;
# do not try to add a regex here, nothing reads one.
PASSWORD_MIN_LENGTH=8


# ── Login lockout ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Unchanged. This is the per-ACCOUNT axis; AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX is the per-IP axis.
# A distributed guessing attack against one known email address is invisible to a
# per-IP limit, which is the gap these two close (env.js:156-165). The lock is
# temporary on purpose: a permanent one would let anyone who knows a user's email
# address disable that account at will.
LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
LOGIN_LOCKOUT_MINUTES=15


# ── Bootstrap Super Admin (created by the seeder) ────────────────────────────

# MUST DIFFER FROM DEV. This is the only account that cannot be created through
# the application (seeders/04-super-admin.js:3-12), so these four values decide
# who owns the platform. Set them BEFORE the first `npm run db:seed`; the seeder
# creates the account once and never rewrites it afterwards (seeder:46-62), so a
# value corrected later has no effect and the account must be fixed in the
# database or a new one seeded under a different email/username.
SUPER_ADMIN_NAME=Platform Owner
SUPER_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
SUPER_ADMIN_USERNAME=superadmin

# <<< REQUIRED — choose a real password, do not generate-and-forget: a human has
# to type this one. >>>
# Left empty, which is safe here for a non-obvious reason worth stating: empty
# makes env.js:177 fall back to the literal `SuperAdmin@123`, which is exactly the
# string `INSECURE_DEFAULT_PASSWORD` at seeders/04-super-admin.js:21, and under
# NODE_ENV=production the seeder THROWS rather than warns (seeder:64-70). So an
# unfilled value cannot quietly seed a known-password platform owner — the seed
# run fails instead. Quote it if it contains `#`.
SUPER_ADMIN_PASSWORD=


# ── Rate limiting (SRS §24) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Unchanged defaults. Tune only with evidence from the access log. Two properties
# of these numbers decide whether they mean anything, and neither is obvious from
# the key names.
#
# WHAT EACH ONE COUNTS. `clientKey()` (src/middlewares/rateLimit.js:107-118) keys on
# the authenticated user when a request has one and on the client address
# otherwise, so the three limits are not all the same kind of number:
#   - RATE_LIMIT_MAX is `apiLimiter`, mounted globally at src/app.js:283, which is
#     before the authentication boundary at app.js:347-348 — so it always keys on
#     IP;
#   - AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX is `authLimiter` on the public auth routes, likewise
#     pre-authentication, so it also keys on IP. Successful requests are not
#     counted (rateLimit.js:222), so it bounds FAILED credential attempts, which is
#     what makes 20 a usable number rather than a lockout of ordinary staff;
#   - AI_RATE_LIMIT_MAX is `aiLimiter`, mounted inside the AI router
#     (src/modules/ai/ai.routes.js:132, :142, :155), which sits BELOW that boundary
#     — so it keys on `user:<id>` (rateLimit.js:116). It is 60 per authenticated
#     user per window, and TRUST_PROXY has no bearing on it at all.
# "Per client IP" therefore describes the first two only, and only if TRUST_PROXY
# is right.
#
# WHERE THEY ARE COUNTED, and this is the one that quietly voids the others. The
# store is `express-rate-limit`'s in-memory one, so the counters live inside each
# node process — rateLimit.js:29-32 states it: a single-process deployment (SRS §3)
# is exactly counted, "behind N workers the effective ceiling is N × the configured
# limit". Run the API at 4 PM2 instances and AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=20 becomes 80
# failed credential attempts per window across the four, with no warning in any log
# and no key in this file to say so. THAT — not only the per-process cache
# described under CACHE_DRIVER — is why the API app stays at `instances: 1` in fork
# mode. A shared counter would need `rate-limit-redis`, which is neither a
# dependency of this project nor a technology SRS §27 lists, so scaling out is a
# decision to be taken with this paragraph in front of you.
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MINUTES=15
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=1000
AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=20
AI_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=60

# MUST DIFFER FROM DEV (empty, which env.js:84 turns into `false`).
# `1` = trust exactly one reverse proxy hop. Set this the moment nginx is in
# front of the app, and set it to `1`, not `true`.
#
# The limiter keys on `req.ip` and `app.set('trust proxy', ...)` runs before it
# (src/app.js:244-245), so this decides what an IP even is:
#   - left at the default behind nginx: every request appears to come from the
#     proxy's address, so all users worldwide share ONE 1000-request bucket and
#     the whole platform rate-limits itself off the air;
#   - set to `true` (trust everything): a caller can put any address in
#     X-Forwarded-For and mint a fresh quota per request, which makes
#     AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=20 above worth exactly nothing against a credential
#     stuffing run.
# `1` means "take the last hop's X-Forwarded-For entry, which nginx wrote itself".
# Accepted forms are Express's own (env.js:82-95): a hop count, a boolean, or a
# subnet/name list such as `loopback` or `10.0.0.0/8`.
TRUST_PROXY=1


# ── Uploads (SRS §24 "File Upload security") ─────────────────────────────────

# Relative paths are resolved against the backend directory (env.js:189-191), so
# the default puts uploaded files INSIDE the application tree. That is fine for a
# deploy that updates in place. If deployment replaces the directory (a release
# folder + symlink scheme), point this at an absolute path outside the release —
# otherwise every previously uploaded document is orphaned by the next deploy
# while the database rows still reference them. The stored paths are relative, so
# moving the directory and re-pointing UPLOAD_DIR is enough; the rows do not need
# rewriting.
#
# WHEREVER YOU PUT IT, DO NOT LET NGINX SERVE IT. There is no `express.static`
# anywhere in this application: every stored file is handed back through
# `src/utils/fileResponse.js`, which checks the caller's permission on the owning
# record, re-checks that the path's first segment is the caller's own
# `school-<id>` (fileResponse.js:142-145) and sets `Cache-Control: private, no-store`
# (fileResponse.js:200). A `location /uploads/ { alias ...; }` block in nginx
# bypasses all three at once and turns every student document into a public URL
# that a shared proxy is then also free to cache.
UPLOAD_DIR=storage/uploads

# Unchanged, and load-bearing for the nginx configuration. This is the SERVER
# backstop and it always wins: the effective ceiling is
# `min(plan limit, MAX_UPLOAD_MB)` (src/middlewares/upload.js:229), so a school's
# plan can only LOWER this number, never raise it. No seeder in this repository
# creates a plan, so on a fresh deployment there is no plan limit at all and this
# key is the only ceiling in play.
#
# The figure is PER FILE, not per request, and that distinction is what the nginx
# number has to be derived from:
#   - 10 MB = 10485760 bytes (`MEGABYTE = 1024 * 1024` at upload.js:79;
#     `maxBytes = Math.floor(maxMb * MEGABYTE)` at upload.js:239);
#   - multer is handed maxBytes + 1 = 10485761 (upload.js:286-287), one byte above
#     on purpose, so a file sitting exactly on the ceiling is refused by the
#     application's own check and not by multer's, and the caller gets the JSON
#     error rather than a multer exception;
#   - a multipart request may carry MORE THAN ONE such file. `uploadSingle` pins
#     `files: 1` (upload.js:486), but `uploadArray` sets `files: count`
#     (upload.js:524), defaulting to the profile's `maxFiles` — which is 10 for
#     STUDENT_DOCUMENT (src/config/constants.js:830, from
#     `UPLOAD_MAX_FILES_DEFAULT = 10` at constants.js:788).
#
# Today no route calls the multi-file path. `grep -rn uploadArray src/` returns
# eight lines and not one of them is a route: its own definition and internal uses
# (upload.js:503, :504, :507, :514, :555), the middleware barrel that re-exports it
# (src/middlewares/index.js:66, :112), and a passing mention in a comment
# (src/middlewares/sanitize.js:197). No `*.routes.js` file names it at all. So the
# largest body any wired route
# can legitimately produce is ONE 10 MB file plus multipart framing, and
# `deploy/nginx/msms.conf` sizes the upload locations at `client_max_body_size 12m`
# on that basis (with 1m as the server-wide default). That is a routing accident,
# not a guarantee: THE MOMENT A ROUTE CALLS `uploadArray`, recompute it — on the
# STUDENT_DOCUMENT profile the ceiling becomes 10 × 10 MB in one request and 12m is
# an order of magnitude short. Change MAX_UPLOAD_MB here and you must change the
# nginx figure in the same commit.
# If nginx is set below the real figure it answers with its own HTML 413 and the
# caller never sees the application's JSON error carrying the limit details
# (upload.js:143-146).
#
# A wrong VALUE here fails in two different places, and only one of them is at
# runtime:
#   - a NON-NUMBER never reaches a request. `num()` throws at env.js:42-44 on the
#     first `require('./config/env')`, so the process refuses to start and names
#     the key in the message.
#   - `0` or a negative parses fine and is caught per request at upload.js:181-189,
#     which logs "Upload ceiling resolved to zero from server configuration" and
#     returns a 500 on every upload while the rest of the API looks healthy.
MAX_UPLOAD_MB=10


# ── Caching (SRS §25) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Keep `memory` and keep REDIS_URL empty. Two independent reasons:
#   1. SRS §27 does not list Redis, and states verbatim that no deployment
#      technology beyond its list is introduced.
#   2. `redis` is not a dependency in backend/package.json, so there is no Redis
#      client to load even if it were.
#
# Setting CACHE_DRIVER=redis anyway does NOT produce a diagnostic, and this is the
# part worth reading twice. `initCache()` opens with
# `if (config.cache.driver !== 'redis' || !config.cache.redisUrl) return;`
# (src/config/cache.js:118). With REDIS_URL empty — which is how this file ships and
# what the paragraph above tells you to keep — that guard returns FIRST: the
# `require('redis')` at cache.js:121 never runs, the catch at cache.js:127-129 never
# fires, and "Redis cache unavailable" is never written anywhere. The result is
# total silence and an in-memory cache. Only if a URL is ALSO supplied does
# execution reach cache.js:121, throw on the missing module, and get caught and
# logged as a warning at cache.js:128 — still falling back to memory. Neither path
# gives you a cache, and only the second leaves evidence. Treat this key as
# verify-by-reading-the-file, not verify-by-log.
#
# Consequence to be aware of: the memory cache is per-process, so running more than
# one API process means each has its own copy and an invalidation in one is not
# seen by the others. That is one of two reasons to keep PM2 at a single API
# instance — the other, and the sharper one, is that the rate-limit counters are
# per-process too, so N instances multiply every limit by N. See the Rate limiting
# section above.
CACHE_DRIVER=memory
REDIS_URL=
CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=300


# ── Queue (SRS §25 "Queue system"). memory | database ────────────────────────

# Keep `memory`. `database` is accepted by env.js:202 but changes NOTHING: the
# queue is always a `MemoryQueue` instance (src/config/queue.js:107) and
# QUEUE_DRIVER is only echoed back as a label by `queueStats()`
# (src/config/queue.js:143). Setting it to `database` would make the monitoring
# surface report a durable queue that does not exist. There is no jobs table and
# §29/§35 forbid adding one — src/jobs/worker.js:12-36 records why at length, and
# the durability answer is the cron reconciliation sweeps below, not this key.
QUEUE_DRIVER=memory

# Unchanged. Bounds how many queued handlers run concurrently INSIDE the API
# process (queue.js:47), so it competes with request handling for the same event
# loop. Raise it only if the box has cores to spare.
QUEUE_CONCURRENCY=3


# ── Mail (SRS §7 email verification, §23 notifications) ──────────────────────

# MUST DIFFER FROM DEV (`log`). Only `log` and `smtp` exist
# (src/services/mailService.js:120-136); any other value is rejected at first send
# (mailService.js:137) and — note — a delivery failure is logged and swallowed,
# never propagated into the request (mailService.js:138-142). So a broken mail
# configuration does not fail loudly: password resets and verification emails just
# never arrive, and the only evidence is an "Email could not be sent" line in the
# error log. Check for it after the first deploy.
MAIL_DRIVER=smtp

# <<< REQUIRED when MAIL_DRIVER=smtp. >>> mailService.js:58-60 throws if the host
# is empty, and per the note above that throw is caught and logged, not surfaced.
MAIL_HOST=

# The port and the TLS flag are a PAIR and the pairing is documented at
# mailService.js:67-73: `secure` means TLS from the first byte, which is port 465.
# Port 587 starts in the clear and upgrades via STARTTLS, which nodemailer does by
# itself — so `MAIL_SECURE=false` on 587 is NOT an unencrypted connection, and
# forcing `true` there fails the handshake. Use 587/false or 465/true; nothing else.
MAIL_PORT=587
MAIL_SECURE=false

# <<< REQUIRED when MAIL_DRIVER=smtp — the relay's own credentials, not generated
# here. >>> Quote the password if it contains `#`.
MAIL_USER=
MAIL_PASSWORD=

# MUST DIFFER FROM DEV (`no-reply@msms.local`). `.local` is not a deliverable
# domain; most relays reject an envelope sender they are not authorised for, and
# the rejection is swallowed as above. Use an address on a domain whose SPF/DKIM
# authorises this relay.
MAIL_FROM="Multi-School Management System <no-reply@example.com>"


# ── AI module (SRS §21) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Two valid values, `mock` and `anthropic` (src/ai/index.js:56-59). The name is
# NOT validated at boot — env.js accepts any string and the error only appears at
# first use (ai/index.js:70-76). With `anthropic`, env.js:286-288 does require the
# key under NODE_ENV=production, so an unset key aborts the boot rather than
# failing later inside a request.
# If the AI features are not being used in this deployment, set `AI_DRIVER=mock`
# and leave the key empty — that is the supported way to boot without a key.
AI_DRIVER=anthropic
AI_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# <<< REQUIRED when AI_DRIVER=anthropic — obtained from the provider's console,
# not generated locally. >>>
# Left empty so the boot fails loudly (env.js:286-288) instead of every AI request
# failing at ai/anthropic.js:61 one at a time in production.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=


# ── Payment gateways (SRS §13.2 — plugin based) ──────────────────────────────

# Unchanged unless a school should not be offered a method. Despite the key's
# name these are payment METHOD names. `assertMethodEnabled()`
# (src/services/paymentGatewayService.js:193) is the refusal; it delegates to
# `isMethodEnabled()` at :182, and :183 is the line that actually reads this list.
# Removing an entry here stops new payments by that method; it does not touch
# payments already recorded against it.
PAYMENT_GATEWAYS=cash,bank_transfer,manual_payment,wallet,online_gateway
ONLINE_GATEWAY_KEY=online_gateway
ONLINE_GATEWAY_LABEL=Online Gateway

# <<< REQUIRED only if `online_gateway` stays in PAYMENT_GATEWAYS above — these
# come from the payment provider, they are not generated here. >>>
# All three default to `''` (env.js:234-236) and nothing asserts them at boot:
# `isConfigured()` (paymentGatewayService.js:252-255) exists to report the state
# but is called from nowhere in `src/`, so an unconfigured gateway is discovered at
# the first real payment attempt and not before.
#
# Filling these three is NOT sufficient on its own, and that is worth knowing
# before a school is promised card payments: NO gateway adapter ships with this
# system. SRS §13.2 says "Online Gateway" and "plugin-based" and names no provider,
# and paymentGatewayService.js:24-32 records why none was invented rather than
# guessed at. So the first online payment fails at paymentGatewayService.js:153-171
# with a 422 and `PAYMENT_GATEWAY_NOT_CONFIGURED`, whose own message names both
# halves of what is missing: "register an adapter via
# paymentGatewayService.register() and set ONLINE_GATEWAY_* in the environment"
# (:167). These keys are the second half only.
#
# If no provider is contracted yet, remove `online_gateway` from PAYMENT_GATEWAYS
# rather than leaving these blank and advertising a method that cannot complete.
# The webhook secret is what proves an inbound callback came from the provider;
# treat it with the same care as a JWT secret.
ONLINE_GATEWAY_API_KEY=
ONLINE_GATEWAY_SECRET=
ONLINE_GATEWAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=


# ── Logging & monitoring (SRS §26) ───────────────────────────────────────────

# Consider `warn` instead of `info` on this deployment, and know what you lose.
# morgan is piped into winston and logs every request at `info`, not at winston's
# `http` level — deliberately, because npm levels put `http` (3) below `info` (2)
# and it would otherwise be discarded (src/app.js:208-211). So `LOG_LEVEL=warn`
# turns off the application's per-request log entirely, which is only acceptable
# because nginx's own access log already covers it (the reasoning app.js:210-211
# gives). `info` keeps both and doubles the disk churn.
# Remember NODE_ENV=production removed the console transport (logger.js:44):
# whatever level is set here, LOG_DIR is the ONLY place these lines land.
# Volume is a real consideration and not a theoretical one: rotation is by date
# with NO size cap (see LOG_RETENTION_DAYS below), so at `info` every request adds
# a line to a file that grows unbounded until midnight — `src/app.js:218` pipes
# each morgan line through `logger.info`. Either size the LOG_DIR filesystem for
# peak requests-per-day × line length, or set `warn` and let nginx's access log
# carry the request record, which is exactly the trade app.js:210-211 sanctions.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Relative, so resolved against the backend directory (env.js:242-244) — the same
# release-directory caveat as UPLOAD_DIR applies, and it matters more here: these
# files are PART OF the monitoring surface §27 asks for, and losing them at each
# deploy means losing the record of what happened before the deploy. An absolute
# path outside the release tree is the safer choice.
#
# Part of, not all of — setting this key does not discharge §27's "Monitoring"
# item. The other two inputs, neither of which is configured from this file:
#   - `GET <API_PREFIX>/health` and `<API_PREFIX>/health/ready`
#     (src/modules/system/system.routes.js:22-23). They are mounted above the
#     authentication boundary, so an uptime check can poll them unauthenticated;
#     that is what an external monitor should watch, not a log file.
#   - PM2's own process facilities — restart count, memory, `pm2 status`. Note
#     `pm2 logs` shows NOTHING from the API under NODE_ENV=production, because
#     logger.js:44 adds the console transport only when not production (see
#     NODE_ENV above). PM2's log files will hold startup and crash output only.
#
# THIS VALUE REACHES ONLY THE API PROCESS UNDER PM2. `deploy/pm2/ecosystem.config.js:502`
# puts `LOG_DIR: 'storage/logs/cron'` in the cron app's `env` block, and per the
# header, dotenv does not override what PM2 has already set (env.js:22). So the
# scheduler writes to `backend/storage/logs/cron/` — relative, resolved against the
# backend directory by env.js:242-244 — no matter what is set here, deliberately,
# so two resident processes do not fight over one `combined-<date>.log`. Two
# consequences: point an external log collector at BOTH directories, and understand
# that moving this key to an absolute path outside the release tree, as recommended
# above, does nothing for the cron process. Changing that means editing
# ecosystem.config.js:502 as well, in the same commit.
#
# WRITABILITY IS NOT CHECKED AT BOOT, despite what the startup sequence suggests.
# logger.js:17 is `fs.mkdirSync(config.logging.dir, { recursive: true })`, and with
# `recursive: true` Node returns successfully on a directory that already exists
# without ever testing whether this process can write into it. A `storage/logs`
# created by root during a release — or the absolute path recommended above,
# created by root — therefore sails past that line as the non-root PM2 user, and
# the failure surfaces later from inside the winston transports instead. Only a
# directory that cannot be CREATED aborts the boot there. Prove writability by hand
# before the first start:
#
#     sudo -u <the PM2 user> touch <LOG_DIR>/.probe && rm <LOG_DIR>/.probe
LOG_DIR=storage/logs

# Unchanged. Passed straight to winston-daily-rotate-file as `maxFiles: '30d'` with
# `zippedArchive: true` on both transports (src/config/logger.js:26-42), so rotation
# and pruning are handled in-process. Do NOT also point logrotate at these files:
# two rotators on one file set is how the open file handle ends up writing to a
# deleted inode.
#
# What this does NOT bound: rotation is by DATE and pruning is by AGE. Neither
# transport sets `maxSize` — the error transport (logger.js:27-34) sets `level`,
# `dirname`, `filename`, `datePattern`, `maxFiles` and `zippedArchive`, and the
# combined one (logger.js:35-41) sets the same six minus `level`, so it inherits
# the logger's own, which logger.js:53 takes from LOG_LEVEL — and env.js exposes no
# key that could add a size cap. So the current day's `combined-<date>.log` grows without
# limit whatever this value is, and `zippedArchive` only compresses days that have
# already rolled. Nothing in this file can cap a single day; that is a disk-sizing
# decision, and it is worth making deliberately because a full LOG_DIR filesystem
# also breaks the thing that most needs to survive it — the backup task deletes its
# own zero-byte dump and throws (databaseBackup.js:173-176), on the cron schedule,
# writing the failure into the very directory that filled up.
LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=30


# ── Backup (SRS §26 "Backup Retention") ──────────────────────────────────────

# Same relative-path caveat as above, with the sharpest consequence of the three:
# a backup directory inside the release tree is destroyed by the deploy that most
# needs it. Point this at a path on separate storage, and copy the dumps off the
# host — a backup living only on the machine it backs up is not a backup.
BACKUP_DIR=storage/backups
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# MUST DIFFER FROM DEV. The development .env on this machine holds
# `C:/xampp/mysql/bin/mysqldump.exe`, a Windows path that cannot exist on the
# Linux target. Copying dev's value here makes the backup task fail with the
# ENOENT that databaseBackup.js:108-111 reports as
# `could not run mysqldump at "..."` — and it fails on the cron schedule, quietly,
# until someone reads the error log.
#
# The bare name below relies on PM2's inherited PATH resolving it. PM2's
# environment can be more minimal than a login shell's, so verify on the target
# and prefer the absolute path it prints:
#
#     command -v mysqldump
#
# The dump runs as DB_USER. Its argument list is built at databaseBackup.js:53-62
# and is, in full:
#
#     --host=<DB_HOST> --port=<DB_PORT> --user=<DB_USER> --single-transaction
#     --routines --triggers --default-character-set=utf8mb4 <DB_NAME>
#
# That last flag matters at RESTORE time, not at dump time: the file is written as
# utf8mb4, so restore it with `mysql --default-character-set=utf8mb4` or every
# non-ASCII name in it comes back mangled. The password is passed via MYSQL_PWD in
# the child's environment rather than on the command line, because an argv is
# readable by any other process on the machine (databaseBackup.js:44-50, :64-66);
# an empty password is deleted from the child environment rather than passed as ""
# (databaseBackup.js:66).
#
# THE PRIVILEGE THIS NEEDS — read it before the first night, not after. There is no
# `--no-tablespaces` in that argument list. On MySQL 8.0.21 and later, mysqldump
# without that flag reads tablespace information from INFORMATION_SCHEMA and so
# requires the GLOBAL `PROCESS` privilege; without it the dump exits non-zero with
# an "Access denied; you need (at least one of) the PROCESS privilege" error, which
# databaseBackup.js:98 turns into `mysqldump exited <n>: ...` in the error log and
# nowhere else. `PROCESS` cannot be scoped to a database, so the
# `GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON msms.*` recommended under DB_USER above does NOT include
# it. Nothing about this failure is visible from the API.
#
# Two ways out. This file recommends (b), and (a) is what unblocks you today:
#
#   (a) Grant it. No code change:
#
#           GRANT PROCESS ON *.* TO 'msms'@'localhost';
#           FLUSH PRIVILEGES;
#
#       Know the cost before typing it: PROCESS is server-wide and lets this
#       account read the currently-executing statement text of every other session
#       on the instance through SHOW PROCESSLIST. Where MySQL serves only this
#       application that is a small widening of an account that already owns the
#       whole database. On a shared instance it is not, and (b) is then the only
#       acceptable answer.
#
#   (b) Add `--no-tablespaces` to the argument list at databaseBackup.js:53-62.
#       The dump does not use tablespace information for anything this system
#       restores, and with the flag present the database-scoped grant above is
#       sufficient and no global privilege is needed anywhere. That is the correct
#       fix; it is a CODE change, so it is recorded in the discrepancies at the
#       foot of this file rather than made here.
#
# This is a property of mysqldump and of your server's version, not of this
# repository, and it could NOT be verified while writing this file — no MySQL
# client exists on the machine it was written on. So verify it deliberately, by
# running the backup task alone rather than waiting for 03:00. Two ways, and they
# prove different things:
#
#     npm run db:backup                              # the task, straight
#     npm run cron -- --once --only=database-backup  # the task through the scheduler
#
# The first runs `databaseBackup.js` as its own entry point, prints the file and
# byte count, and exits 1 with `backup failed: ...` if the dump errored. The second
# goes through `runOrdered`, prints a JSON report and exits non-zero if any task in
# it failed; `--only=` is what keeps the other four sweeps (subscriptions,
# notifications, invoices, coupons) from running as a side effect of the test.
# Both write a real dump into BACKUP_DIR and both prune to BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS —
# neither is a dry run.
MYSQLDUMP_PATH=mysqldump


# ── Cron / scheduler ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# LEFT AT `false`, WHICH IS THE SAME AS DEV, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Read this
# before "fixing" it — the scheduler DOES run in this deployment, and turning this
# key on here is what would break the protection, not what turns the sweeps on.
#
# SRS §27 lists Cron Jobs, and without the flag the resident scheduler refuses to
# start (src/jobs/cron.js:255-261, which prints why and exits 1): subscriptions
# never renew or expire, invoices never go overdue, coupons never expire,
# notifications are never dispatched, and the database is never backed up. So
# something must set it. That something is PM2, not this file:
# `deploy/pm2/ecosystem.config.js:501` puts `ENABLE_CRON: 'true'` in the msms-cron
# app's own `env` block, and PM2's value reaches the process ahead of this file
# (env.js:22 calls dotenv without `override` — see the header). The supervised
# scheduler is therefore on, and §27 is satisfied, with this key at `false`.
#
# WHY NOT SET IT HERE AS WELL. Because `backend/.env` is read by every node process
# on the box, and this flag is the only thing standing between the deployment and a
# SECOND resident scheduler. With `true` in this file, an operator who types
# `npm run cron` to "check something" starts a second sweeper against the live
# database and gets no warning: double-notified users, raced subscription renewals,
# duplicate overdue transitions. With `false` here, that hand-typed run refuses and
# prints why, while the PM2 app is unaffected. `ecosystem.config.js:463-472` states
# the same reasoning from the other side, and `deploy/logrotate/msms:500-503` and
# `deploy/monitoring/README.md:445` both depend on it. Setting it `true` here
# silently voids all three.
#
# Flip it to `true` ONLY if you are running the scheduler outside PM2 — which is
# not the shipped design, and means re-reading the two paragraphs above first.
#
# `--once` is not affected either way: cron.js:58-60 says so outright ("running it
# is already the operator saying so"), which is why the backup check recommended
# under MYSQLDUMP_PATH works with this key at `false`.
#
# WHAT READS IT. Only the cron entry point acts on it at runtime (env.js:257 reads
# it into `config.cron.enabled`, cron.js:249 gates on it) — the API process ignores
# it entirely. (`scripts/verify-jobs.js` also reads it, at :226-230, but only to
# assert from both sides that the gate refuses and that its default is off. That is
# a test, not a second consumer.) The other hazard, which no value of this key
# addresses, is running the cron app with more than one PM2 instance or in cluster
# mode: two schedulers on one database double-notify and race the renewals, which
# is the failure cron.js:56-60 exists to prevent. Keep the cron app at
# `instances: 1`, fork mode.
#
# AND: the boot-time assertions this file leans on do not protect the scheduler.
# `assertRuntimeConfig()` is called from src/server.js:86 and from nowhere else;
# cron.js requires `../config/env` at cron.js:65 and never asserts. So the msms-cron
# app — which PM2 switches on regardless of this key — starts against the production
# database with empty JWT secrets, an empty DB_PASSWORD and a placeholder mail
# configuration, and says nothing, while the API next to it refuses to boot. Do not
# rely on noticing the API's refusal first. Start `npm start` once by hand and let
# it pass BEFORE `pm2 start`; that run is the only check these values get.
ENABLE_CRON=false

# Leave false, and understand that the value is inert either way. env.js:204 reads
# it into `config.queue.enableWorker` and NOTHING in the codebase reads that
# property — `grep -rn "enableWorker\|ENABLE_QUEUE_WORKER" src/` returns env.js:204
# and nothing else. `npm run worker` runs ONE named job inline and exits with that
# job's own outcome (the entry block at src/jobs/worker.js:57-111; `runNow` at
# :101, deliberately not `enqueue`); it is not a resident consumer and this flag
# does not gate it. src/jobs/worker.js:11-36 explains why a resident worker is not
# possible without a jobs table that §29/§35 forbid. Setting this to `true` would
# enable nothing; it would only suggest to the next operator that a queue worker is
# running.
#
# One trap for anyone who goes looking: worker.js:29 refers to a flag called
# `ENABLE_WORKER` and says it "governs" in-process queueing. No such key exists —
# not in env.js, not in `.env.example`, not here. The name in this file
# (ENABLE_QUEUE_WORKER) is the one env.js actually reads, and it governs nothing.
ENABLE_QUEUE_WORKER=false


# ── CSRF (applies to cookie-authenticated browser requests) ──────────────────

# Unchanged, and must stay true. env.js:168 already defaults it to true outside
# tests; it is spelled out here so nobody sets it false to "fix" a CORS problem —
# the double-submit token is what stops a third-party page from spending a user's
# refresh cookie, and the `X-CSRF-Token` header is already allowed through the
# preflight (src/app.js:195 — :194 is the comment above it).
CSRF_ENABLED=true

# Unchanged. The name is shared knowledge with the frontend, which reads this
# cookie to echo the value back (src/middlewares/csrf.js:48-52); renaming it here
# without changing the frontend breaks every state-changing request.
CSRF_COOKIE_NAME=msms_csrf


# ── The httpOnly cookie the refresh token travels in (SRS §7 FR-AUTH-003) ────

# Unchanged. Renaming it logs out everyone holding the old cookie — the server
# stops looking for the name their browser is sending.
REFRESH_COOKIE_NAME=msms_refresh


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Discrepancies found while writing this file — recorded rather than fixed,
# because fixing them is a code change and this row is a template.
#
# 1. `.env.example` and `src/config/env.js` AGREE exactly: 73 keys on each side,
#    same names, nothing read-but-undocumented, nothing documented-but-unread.
#    The counts were compared mechanically, not by eye.
#
# 2. `DEBUG` is read at src/database/cli.js:194 (`if (process.env.DEBUG)` — prints
#    a stack trace when a migration fails) and appears in NEITHER env.js NOR
#    `.env.example`. It is the one environment variable this system consults that
#    is documented nowhere. It is not added to this file: it is an interactive
#    debugging switch for the migration CLI, set ad hoc on the command line, and
#    putting it in the persistent environment would only make it permanent.
#
# 3. Three keys env.js reads that no runtime code consumes, all noted at their
#    entries above: APP_URL (env.js:107 → `config.app.url`, never read),
#    ENABLE_QUEUE_WORKER (env.js:204 → `config.queue.enableWorker`, never read),
#    and QUEUE_DRIVER (env.js:202, used only as a display label at
#    src/config/queue.js:143). All three were re-checked by grep across `src/` for
#    this revision and all three are still unread. They are kept because env.js
#    reads them and because removing a key from one file and not the other breaks
#    the key-set comparison in the header, which is the thing keeping these two
#    files honest with each other.
#
# 4. Unrelated to production but found by the same sweep: 22 verification scripts
#    under backend/scripts/ set `process.env.CACHE_TTL` (verify-addons.js:22 is
#    one), while env.js:198 reads `CACHE_TTL_SECONDS`. Every one of those pins
#    does nothing, and several of the scripts' own headers call the pin
#    "load-bearing". The suites pass anyway, so nothing depended on it — but a
#    suite that believes it has pinned a TTL and has not is a trap for whoever
#    next writes an assertion that really does need one.
#
# 5. `mysqldump` is invoked without `--no-tablespaces` (argv at
#    src/jobs/tasks/databaseBackup.js:53-62). On MySQL 8.0.21+ that makes the
#    nightly backup require the GLOBAL `PROCESS` privilege, which cannot be
#    granted database-scoped — so the otherwise-correct
#    `GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON msms.*` for DB_USER is not enough and the task fails
#    on a schedule nobody is watching. RECOMMENDED FIX: add `--no-tablespaces` to
#    that argument list, after which no global privilege is needed at all. Until
#    that lands, the operator's workaround is `GRANT PROCESS ON *.*` — see the
#    MYSQLDUMP_PATH entry above for both, and for what PROCESS costs.
#
# 6. src/jobs/worker.js:29 names a flag called `ENABLE_WORKER` and says it governs
#    in-process queueing. That key exists nowhere — not in env.js, not in
#    `.env.example`, not here. The real key is ENABLE_QUEUE_WORKER (env.js:204),
#    and it governs nothing either (item 3). Same class as item 4: a comment that
#    describes a control which is not wired. Noted at the ENABLE_QUEUE_WORKER entry
#    so an operator who greps for the name in that comment is not left guessing.
#
